              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where can I find an anti-gravity treadmill near me?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Physiotherapy and sports medicine clinics are the most common location, particularly those with a running or orthopaedic specialism, along with hospital rehabilitation departments, university athletic facilities and some specialist recovery studios. The main manufacturer publishes a searchable location finder, which is far quicker than ringing round clinics.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What does running on one feel like?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most people describe floating or bounding — your legs move through a normal gait but the landing is far softer than it should be at that speed. The chamber inflating around your hips feels odd for about thirty seconds. People routinely run at paces and durations impossible for them at full weight, which is the point and also why supervision matters.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a cheaper alternative to an anti-gravity treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aquatic treadmills are the closest relative and far more available, though water adds drag so the gait is less normal. Deep-water running needs only a pool. For home use, incline walking is the most practical substitute — 3 mph at 12% grade costs roughly what running 5 mph costs, at a walking impact load, which is a version of the same trade.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes and Misconceptions About Anti-Gravity Treadmills</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The technology is unusual enough that it attracts some persistent misunderstandings, and one of them is a genuine injury risk.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming reduced impact means you cannot overdo it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Joint loading is reduced; your cardiovascular system, your tendons above the seal and your overall fatigue are not. The usual pain signal that limits a run is muted, which makes it easy to do far more than the tissue is ready for. That is precisely why the machine is used under supervision, and it is the most important thing to understand about it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Thinking it works like a harness or a pool</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A harness lifts from one point and changes your posture; water adds drag and slows your legs. Pressure-based unweighting is distributed across the lower body, so the gait stays close to normal while the load falls. That preservation of movement pattern is the whole reason it is useful for return-to-run work rather than just for maintaining fitness.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trying to buy one for a home gym</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The cost is in car territory, the footprint is substantial, and the pressure system needs calibration and servicing by trained technicians rather than a general treadmill engineer. More to the point, the value is in prescribed progression rather than in the hardware. Book sessions at a clinic; put the equipment budget into a treadmill with real incline.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Seeking it out instead of asking a clinician</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Not every lower-limb injury benefits from unweighted running, and the progression — starting percentage, how fast to add load, when to stop — is the actual treatment. Ask your physiotherapist or surgeon whether it is appropriate and available; it is frequently bundled into a course of treatment rather than sold as a standalone session.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

